[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This might look like a normal digital camera, but it\'s actually an instant camera with removable, reusable digital film. And the photos don\'t even need a battery to keep their image. I made six of these cartridges, and once the camera is finished, I want to find out if I can'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually make a set of photos worth keeping. Digital photos are incredibly convenient, but they also kind of disappear the moment you take them. I wanted something physical again without actually printing anything. So, I'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'started with the simplest version I could make. It\'s a Raspberry Pi, a camera module, and an e-paper display. With this setup, I could send pictures from the camera to the screen. That\'s me, by the way. Hello. The display does have a connector, but it\'s way too'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fiddly to swap quickly. And if this is going to feel like digital film, changing cartridges has to be effortless. There is, however, another way. We can use our own connector on this PCB. I considered these pogo spring connectors, and you can just press them'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together, and they will make an electrical connection. However, then I would need a locking mechanism to prevent it from springing back up. Also, these are like really expensive at €3 there. So, sometimes the simplest connector is the best one. These basic'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'male and female headers cost next to nothing, and unlike the pogo pins, they also hold the cartridge firmly in place. With the cartridge problem solved, I could make the camera portable by adding a battery. And then I figured out the'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best orientation for all the parts, so I could package everything into a compact enclosure. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I designed the cartridge in a way where'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you don\'t need any screws to assemble it. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, I\'m just going to replace all this'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by the new connector that we made. &gt;&gt; And here I probably short-circuited something. &gt;&gt; And does it work? &gt;&gt; No, it doesn\'t work. It just took me an hour of reinstalling, but uh here we are. And it works again.'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Which means it\'s time to put all the electronics into the 3D printed enclosure. For this prototype, I mostly used off-the-shelf boards, but if I wanted to make this into a proper compact device, the obvious next step would be putting everything onto a'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'custom PCB, which is conveniently something &gt;&gt; PCBWay, the sponsor of this video, can make for you. On their website, you can upload your design, go through the steps, and you get an instant price quote. Thanks for sponsoring the video, PCBWay.'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The idea was to screw this cap on, but I forgot. So, now we\'re just going to glue it. All this time I\'ve been missing a grip'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to pull the cartridge back out, so I want to try a Game Boy-style grip. Perfect. [music] This feels like Pokémon Yellow so badly. But then suddenly, it stopped working.'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; E-paper busy timeout. &gt;&gt; So, sometimes it works and sometimes it doesn\'t. So, I\'m going to inspect the wiring. Even like this, it\'s still working and writing to the cartridge. I couldn\'t find anything on the camera'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'side, so &gt;&gt; Maybe it\'s in the cartridge itself. &gt;&gt; The issue turned out to be a solder connection on the e-paper module. Turns out every single module had the exact same issue. One solder pad just refused to take solder. Only after adding a lot'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more heat did it finally stick. So, for those of you wondering, I paid €8.69 for each of the display modules. [music] Not exactly cheap, but an original Instax film costs €1 and you can only'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write that once. But all the cartridges work, and I made this little stand to display them on. At this point, you might be wondering how I process the images the camera takes. I grab some test photos made with this camera'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'module. Because the display only has four colors, I had to write a small Python [music] script to reduce every photo to black, white, red, and yellow. I experimented with contrast, brightness, saturation, sharpness, and'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finally dithering to get a different look. And then I move the settings to the Raspberry Pi. So, the camera is finally finished. Now'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
